  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Arranging team batting orders with a fixed position and full student rows</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>(a)</b> A sports team has \({@pn1@}\) players. How many different batting orders are possible if the captain must always bat last?</p>
<p>Number of batting orders = [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p><b>(b)</b> \({@pn2@}\) students are arranged in a single row for a class photo, with no restrictions on position. How many different arrangements are possible?</p>
<p>Number of arrangements = [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part (a): Batting order with the captain fixed last</b><br>
Because the captain's position is fixed in the last spot, only the remaining \({@pn1@} - 1 = {@prem1@}\) players need to be arranged in the remaining \({@prem1@}\) spots:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle ({@pn1@} - 1)! = {@prem1@}! = {@exp1@} = {@ta1@}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \text{Number of batting orders} = {@ta1@} }\)</p>

<p><b>Part (b): Arranging all {@pn2@} students with no restrictions</b><br>
When arranging all \(n = {@pn2@}\) distinct students in a row with no fixed positions, the number of arrangements is \(n!\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle {@pn2@}! = {@exp2@} = {@ta2@}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \text{Number of arrangements} = {@ta2@} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
